#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497436} :activity/id #uuid "90858bbd-4be9-59a1-90ae-3a0211b98927" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050814 :topic/id #uuid "68ad7925-6faa-546c-899e-dac0bbfac1ef" :topic/math-academy-id 6722} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497420 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497410 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "462d45d4-2587-5767-bbda-82dc90eb9b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496809} {:answer/id #uuid "480be999-cd10-5a4f-83f5-02eb7f7b0275" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496819} {:answer/id #uuid "4ad1a635-ce6b-5e3d-a212-6ca66dbb9f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0f4e40-8515-583c-aa83-8de5f9788688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497078}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496826} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a94d15-fe13-5d85-9f7d-69d7a8a6ed69" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ed7b5966-6097-5c94-afc7-cde3e72271b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344323" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+2y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+B\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{2}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{2}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 5 & =-A\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0664f390-8c5d-5111-9629-741058ac23ee"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497436 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cafa24e-4543-5b34-90d7-30a196dea194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372868} {:answer/id #uuid "e11913eb-e3e1-5f57-a56a-942b4c1c6e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372869} {:answer/id #uuid "677af0cd-8fcf-515a-9ae8-5589651ec3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372870} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca9f35e-fe96-5630-bbab-c22309b7bc81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372868} :answer-field/id #uuid "426794d2-6862-5438-ba72-af2a254821f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28c90549-a363-5df9-b9c5-c5f06fe88f4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344281" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-1,1]$, where $f$ is $2$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $2$-periodic on $[-1,1]$, we have $L=1$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{1})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{1}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5219f94f-df72-53fc-a578-2400b0bec682" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca1a5ef5-d795-51cc-943c-7a342a6b79ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372886} {:answer/id #uuid "e0d3a205-6dc6-5c9e-bac4-a249e8ab4de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372887} {:answer/id #uuid "2853c5b9-37c6-5227-b945-7d1f193fd0b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372888} {:answer/id #uuid "83ee3b8e-a567-507f-a915-c7357bb35a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372889}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372889} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b894e19-8672-5d3a-ad64-9899ae78c522" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1435ee5-1c1b-550d-94b4-e30eac47db5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344322" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8y=f(x),\\,{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $1$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+8y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x).\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+B\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+{y}_{p}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}^{′}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)={y}_{c}^{′}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(1)={y}_{c}^{′}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =0+B\\sqrt{8}(1)+{y}_{p}^{′}(0) \\\\ 0 & =B\\sqrt{8}+0 \\\\ B & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x).$\n\n- Use ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ with $B=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})+{y}_{p}^{′}(1) \\\\ 3 & =-A\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})+0 \\\\ A & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{8})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{8}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8acff912-5281-5c53-9ef5-b6eb39e2fc94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497420}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497482 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497355 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "850e4194-b453-541e-be6e-32d1ccb2f133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496930} {:answer/id #uuid "88cf4171-cb9b-50eb-bbf6-945c7832695e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496942} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c84257-de9c-5c9c-a2d5-4b7b4af017c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496993} {:answer/id #uuid "fbab4fd2-4962-567f-9b94-4d8fec410878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497199}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496993} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d8624c8-93b0-5430-b609-5a1fb6fd1e74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4b4d6e1d-9198-53a2-8fea-90e3eb2a7b81" :question/math-academy-id "q-344316" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $2$-periodic and odd. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+3y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=2$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 2 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e7df0d1a-8d1c-5391-8fd4-541fd7cfa273" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03bcde23-8fb4-54be-b4f9-12b3458b95b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496665} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8a9980-7371-5df1-8410-26fa8e6ff9c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496683} {:answer/id #uuid "1989b09c-b976-5029-b925-43a8bcf85f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} {:answer/id #uuid "86a2fd99-6f60-54d4-ba6c-934c44c7e141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496712} :answer-field/id #uuid "48d46346-4c74-57e4-aaa3-613d6a4a40ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f217825-c491-59e7-9325-ea7e5e5d43ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-344285" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, where $f$ is $3$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $3$-periodic on $[-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3}{2}]$, we have $L=\\frac{3}{2}$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2n\\pi x}{3})\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc86aaa6-2771-57b6-8f41-5b9ad203d185" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497482}}] :activity/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496654}]}
